    // The mutant removes that restriction, allowing any address to call requestPrice and emit the event.
    // We detect the difference by calling requestPrice from a fuzzed address (via prank). On the reference, only the optimistic asserter
    // can call; since the optimistic asserter is address(this) in setUp, a prank from any other address will still succeed in mutant
    // (emitting the event) but the reference will still accept the call (because requestPrice is protected) — to make a behavior
    // difference observable via contract getters, we rely on the fact that only the optimistic asserter should be allowed to call
    // requestPrice; however events are not readable via getters. Instead, we use the fact the reference's requestPrice is onlyOptimisticAsserter
    // and thus will reject/prank mismatch when called by an address other than optimistic asserter if the modifier enforced a revert.
    // But since the reference's modifier reverts when msg.sender != optimisticAsserter, we can detect difference by calling requestPrice
    // from a fuzzed caller: on reference the call will revert (caught via try/catch), on mutant it will succeed. Therefore the property is:
    // For all callers (addresses) and inputs, calling requestPrice via a non-optimistic asserter should revert (ok == false) on the reference; the mutant will allow some caller to succeed.

